# ── build outputs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/oarlockd
/oarlock-agent
/oarlockctl
/dist/
packages/*/dist/
# A stale console build lands here when vite is run without the outDir override.
/web/dist/
# The landing sheet is a static build, same reasoning.
/landing/dist/

# Mintlify pages generated from the canonical Markdown. `pnpm docs` writes them.
/mint/generated/
/mint/openapi.yaml
/mint/figures/
/mint/.mintlify/

# TypeScript's incremental build metadata: a cache, and it names absolute paths.
*.tsbuildinfo

# The console's built assets. The *directory* stays (see the .gitkeep inside it):
# `go:embed all:dist` needs something to embed on a clean checkout, so that a build
# without the front-end toolchain still produces a working gateway.
cmd/oarlockd/app/ui/dist/*
!cmd/oarlockd/app/ui/dist/.gitkeep

# The rendered diagrams. The *specifications* beside them are committed and are the
# source; the HTML is 700 KB apiece and most of those bytes are a copy of the Archify
# viewer runtime, three times over. Built web assets are already excluded above on the
# same reasoning — what this repository commits generated is small data (wire vectors,
# condition tables, recording fixtures), not a vendored renderer.
#
# Regenerate with the commands in docs/diagrams/README.md.
docs/diagrams/*.html

# And the evidence `archify visual-check` writes beside an artifact: screenshots at four
# viewports in both themes, plus its receipt and contact sheet. Regenerable, and the
# receipt records a machine's Chrome rather than anything about this project.
docs/diagrams/*.visual-check.*
docs/diagrams/*.png

# Impeccable's per-run scratch: the decision-page transcripts and the build-phase state
# machine. The durable half of that directory — config.json and surfaces/, which carry the
# recorded build path and each surface's direction contract — is committed, because a later
# session reloads them to know what this design already decided.
.impeccable/questions/
.impeccable/build/

# ── dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/

# The BMAD tooling, installed rather than written. Its *output* is committed —
# _bmad-output/ holds the PRD, the epics and the risk registers, which are why the
# code is shaped as it is.
/_bmad/

# ── test and run artefacts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/playwright-report/
/test-results/
/.playwright/
/.cache/
/android/agent-app/.gradle/
/android/agent-app/build/
/android/agent-app/app/build/
/android/agent-app/app/libs/*.aar
/android/agent-app/app/libs/*-sources.jar

# A scratch deployment: private keys (device, operator, host, recording-signing), a
# session database and real recordings. Nothing in it belongs in a repository, and
# examples/ carries the same configuration without the secrets.
/demo/

# The same artefacts, one directory up. A config with relative paths — `oarlockd -config
# demo/oarlock.yaml` run from the repo root rather than from demo/ — resolves them
# against the working directory, so a signing key lands here instead. It is 32 bytes at
# mode 0600 with an innocuous name, next to a hundred files that are meant to be
# committed, which is precisely how a private key gets into a repository.
/oarlock.db
/oarlock.db-shm
/oarlock.db-wal
/recording.key
/recording.key.pub
/hostkey

# Fuzzing corpora that Go writes on a crash are useful locally and are committed
# deliberately when they are a regression case, so this ignores nothing by default.

# ── editors and platforms ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
